    <h1>Gravity Scaling & Inclined Plane Range</h1>

    <div class="container">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>Physics Explanation</h2>
            <p>This simulation demonstrates how gravity scaling affects the motion of a block sliding down an inclined plane.</p>

            <div class="formula-card">
                Range: \( R = v_x \times t = \frac{2v_x v_y}{g} = 4h \cos\theta \sin\theta \)
            </div>

            <p>Notice how the gravity term (g) cancels out! This means:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>On high-gravity planets: The block accelerates faster down the ramp but has less flight time</li>
                <li>On low-gravity planets: The block accelerates slower but has more flight time</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The two effects balance perfectly, resulting in the same range regardless of gravity strength.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Planet Selection</h2>
            <div class="controls">
                <button id="earth" class="active">Earth (9.8 m/s²)</button>
                <button id="moon">Moon (1.6 m/s²)</button>
                <button id="mars">Mars (3.7 m/s²)</button>
                <button id="jupiter">Jupiter (24.8 m/s²)</button>
            </div>

            <div class="slider-container">
                <label for="angle">Incline Angle: <span id="angleValue">30°</span></label>
                <input type="range" id="angle" min="10" max="60" value="30">
            </div>

            <div class="slider-container">
                <label for="height">Ramp Height: <span id="heightValue">4m</span></label>
                <input type="range" id="height" min="2" max="8" value="4" step="0.5">
            </div>

            <button id="resetBtn">Reset Simulation</button>

            <div class="data-panel">
                <div class="data-box">
                    <div class="data-label">Gravity</div>
                    <div class="data-value" id="gravityValue">9.8 m/s²</div>
                </div>
                <div class="data-box">
                    <div class="data-label">Range</div>
                    <div class="data-value" id="rangeValue">3.46 m</div>
                </div>
                <div class="data-box">
                    <div class="data-label">Flight Time</div>
                    <div class="data-value" id="timeValue">1.01 s</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="simulation-container">
            <canvas id="physicsCanvas"></canvas>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Key Observations</h2>
            <div class="planet-info">
                <div class="planet-icon">🌎</div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Earth:</strong> Block accelerates quickly down the ramp but falls rapidly,
                    resulting in shorter flight time.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="planet-info">
                <div class="planet-icon">🌕</div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Moon:</strong> Block slides slowly but stays in the air longer,
                    covering the same horizontal distance.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="planet-info">
                <div class="planet-icon">🔥</div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Jupiter:</strong> Extreme acceleration down the ramp but very short flight time - 
                    again the same range!
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Physics Insights</h2>
            <p>The range independence from gravity comes from energy conservation:</p>
            <p>Initial potential energy: \( E_p = mgh \)</p>
            <p>Kinetic energy at ramp bottom: \( E_k = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = mgh \)</p>
            <p>Thus: \( v = \sqrt{2gh} \)</p>
            <p>The horizontal range calculation then becomes independent of both mass and gravity.</p>
            <p>This holds true for any gravity scaling as long as:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>There's no air resistance</li>
                <li>The landing height equals the ramp bottom height</li>
                <li>Friction is negligible</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
